# Line endings
#
# This repository predates any line-ending policy, so files were committed in
# whatever the contributor's editor produced. Without this file every clone
# behaves differently depending on the contributor's core.autocrlf setting,
# and a one-line change eventually shows up as a whole-file diff.
#
# Policy: Git decides for text files and normalises them to LF in the
# repository. Working copies on Windows still get CRLF, so Visual Studio and
# Notepad see what they expect.
#
# This does NOT rewrite history. Existing commits keep whatever they had; the
# normalisation applies from here on, as files are touched.

# Let Git detect text files and normalise them
* text=auto

# Source
*.c      text
*.cpp    text
*.cxx    text
*.h      text
*.hpp    text
*.cs     text
*.py     text

# Visual Studio project files. These must be CRLF in the working tree or
# Visual Studio will rewrite them on save, producing noise in every diff.
*.sln    text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj        text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj.filters text eol=crlf
*.csproj text eol=crlf
*.props  text eol=crlf
*.targets text eol=crlf

# Resource files: Visual Studio's resource editor writes CRLF
*.rc     text eol=crlf
*.rc2    text eol=crlf
*.def    text eol=crlf

# Windows scripts break when given LF endings
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# OpenHoldem scripts, tablemaps and documentation
*.ohf    text
*.tm     text
*.txt    text
*.md     text
*.ini    text
*.INI    text
*.lyx    text

# Web
*.htm    text
*.html   text
*.css    text
*.js     text
*.xml    text

# Binary: never touch these
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.JPG    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.bmp    binary
*.ico    binary
*.chm    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.lib    binary
*.aps    binary
*.pfb    binary

# Generated resource cache: Visual Studio rewrites it constantly and it is
# not needed to build. Marked binary so it never produces a text diff.
OpenHoldem/OpenHoldem.aps binary
